# ARBITER — environment
#
# Copy to `.env` (which is gitignored) and edit. Every value below has a working
# default, so an EMPTY .env — or none at all — is a valid configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   npm install && npm run seed:demo && npm run dev
#
# Hand out THIS file, never your own `.env`.
#
# TWO NAMES ARE READ, in order: `.env`, then `.env.share`. The second exists so a
# configuration prepared for somebody else works where it lands, instead of needing a
# rename step whose only real function was to be forgotten — a share file sitting
# unread looks exactly like having no credentials at all. `.env` wins when both are
# present, so a shared file never silently replaces a developer's own. The startup
# banner prints which one it read.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. NOTHING AT ALL — the default, and it works
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# With no credentials the product runs. Cases, positions, blind reveal, unanimity,
# the audit and the hash-chained record are pure code and need no model.
#
#   Adjudication  runs against a stub, and every response carries `source: "stub"`
#                 so it can never be read as a model's answer (services/api/probe.ts).
#   Ask & summary return 503 {"error":"no_key"}. These are the only surfaces that
#                 genuinely require a model.
#
# The startup banner tells you which of the two you are in. Leave sections 2 and 3
# blank unless you want live AI.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. LIVE AI — pick ONE provider. Server-side only; never sent to a browser.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The provider is inferred from the MODEL NAME (services/api/interpret.ts): a name
# starting `gemini-` routes to Vertex, anything else to Anthropic. There is no second
# switch to disagree with, so no pair of variables can name a model its provider
# cannot serve.

# --- 2a. Anthropic: the lower-friction path. A key, and nothing else. ---
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# ARBITER_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5

# --- 2b. Gemini: the project's own default. Three ways to authenticate. ---
#
# ADC is the default and the right one for a laptop you own. Each person runs, once:
#     gcloud auth application-default login
#     gcloud services enable aiplatform.googleapis.com
# and sets their OWN project below. Nothing secret goes in this file.
# ARBITER_GCP_PROJECT=your-gcp-project-id

# A KEY, when the model has to be shared. ADC authenticates a PERSON, so it cannot be
# handed to a team - everyone would need their own account on the project. A key is one
# line, and it is still a cloud credential: an `AQ.`-prefixed key is bound to a Google
# Cloud project and bills it. Sufficient on its own; needs no project id beside it.
#
# Sharing one key means sharing one budget, so ARBITER_MODEL_BUDGET below matters more
# once this is in use, not less.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=AQ....

# Which service a key talks to. `vertex` is the default and the only one this codebase
# can actually use.
#
#   vertex     aiplatform.googleapis.com, express mode. Needs aiplatform.googleapis.com
#              enabled on the key's project, and billing linked, or every call is a 403
#              naming that project.
#
#   developer  generativelanguage.googleapis.com. DO NOT USE WITH THIS CODEBASE. That
#              API rejects `additionalProperties: false` with
#              `Unknown name "additionalProperties" ... Cannot find field`, and every
#              schema in services/api sets it - adjudicate, ask, extract, interpret,
#              navigate. It is left reachable because the failure is worth being able
#              to reproduce, not because it is an option: unconstrained calls succeed
#              and the banner reads LIVE, so the deployment looks healthy until the
#              first real adjudication returns 400. It also serves a smaller catalogue
#              (gemini-2.5-flash-lite is a 404 there).
# ARBITER_GEMINI_HOST=vertex

# Alternatives to ADC, for a deployed service rather than a laptop. On Google Cloud
# prefer none of them: attach a service account to the service and the library finds it
# with no key material anywhere. GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_JSON must be ONE line -
# the loader is line-based, and a pretty-printed blob loads as garbage.
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/absolute/path/to/service-account.json
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_JSON={"type":"service_account", ...}

# Model overrides. Defaults: gemini-3.5-flash for adjudication/ask/summary,
# gemini-2.5-flash-lite for short classification calls.
# ARBITER_MODEL=
# ARBITER_ADJUDICATION_MODEL=
# ARBITER_ASK_MODEL=

# Consensus runs per adjudication, capped at 9 in runsFrom(). Each run is a separate
# billed call, so this multiplies spend directly.
# ARBITER_ADJUDICATION_RUNS=3

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. SERVING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Bind address. Loopback unless you say otherwise, because this process terminates
# no TLS — set 0.0.0.0 only behind a proxy that does.
# ARBITER_HOST=127.0.0.1

# API port. Most hosts inject PORT themselves.
# PORT=8787

# The built site, served by the API process itself — which is what puts the website and
# /api on ONE ORIGIN. The client makes same-origin /api calls and no CORS handling
# exists anywhere, so a site served from a different origin than the API is a page that
# fails on its first request.
#
# Leave it UNSET in development. `npm run dev` fronts every surface with the landing
# app's Vite proxy, and a second thing claiming those paths is a conflict, not a
# fallback. Set it in a container, where there is no Vite: the Dockerfile already does.
# Point it at what `npm run site:build` produces.
# ARBITER_STATIC_DIR=apps/landing/dist

# Where the two Vite dev servers proxy /api to, and the public dev port.
# API_PORT=8787
# ARBITER_PORT=5173

# Model calls one account may make per 10 minutes. This is the spend cap: it is what
# makes the paid endpoints safe to expose at all. Per-source is 6x this.
# ARBITER_MODEL_BUDGET=30

# Passages retrieved per Ask.
# ARBITER_ASK_K=

# Python executable used to measure and extract PDFs.
# PYTHON=python

# Create the five demonstration accounts on boot, if there are none. Equivalent to
# `npm run seed:demo`, and meant for a configuration handed to somebody who has not
# read the README: a product that opens on a sign-in screen with nobody to sign in as
# looks broken rather than unseeded.
#
# It runs ONLY into an empty account store, so it cannot appear beside real users and
# cannot resurrect a team that was deliberately deleted. The five share one published
# password. Leave this unset for anything holding real data.
# ARBITER_DEMO_SEED=1

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. STATE — local files by default, Supabase when these are set
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WITHOUT THESE, STATE IS FOUR FILES: results/deliberation-log.jsonl (the record
# itself), results/deliberation-log.jsonl.users.json (the accounts, with their password
# hashes), the invites sidecar beside it, and results/documents/ (the uploaded PDFs).
# That is the default on purpose - `npm test`, `npm run e2e` and CI all run on a
# machine with no database, and a laptop needs nothing installed to work on this.
#
# It is also the arrangement that loses everything on an ephemeral container's next
# redeploy, and that failure is silent: a deployment missing DATABASE_URL looks
# perfectly healthy right up to the moment the record is gone.
#
# APPLY supabase/migrations/0001_init.sql FIRST. Setting DATABASE_URL against an empty
# database gives a service that starts cleanly and then answers every request with a
# missing-table error, which reads as a broken build rather than a missing migration.
#
# Absent ⇒ files. Present ⇒ Postgres. There is no third switch, and the choice is made
# in one place (buildStores() in services/api/stores.ts) rather than by scattered
# checks at call sites.
#
# IN A DEPLOYMENT, USE THE POOLER — port 6543, not the direct 5432. A container that
# restarts, redeploys or scales opens a fresh pool each time, and direct connections
# exhaust a Supabase project's connection limit quickly. Transaction-mode pooling is safe
# for this codebase specifically because the chain append takes `pg_advisory_xact_lock`,
# which is released at COMMIT — the unit the pooler multiplexes on. A session-scoped
# `pg_advisory_lock` would break silently behind a pooler, so that is not a detail to
# "simplify" later. Locally, against `supabase start`, the direct port is fine and there
# is no pooler to use.
#
# Direct  (local / single long-lived process):
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:PASSWORD@db.your-project.supabase.co:5432/postgres
# Pooler  (Railway, Fly, anything that redeploys):
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.your-project:PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres

# TWO CREDENTIALS FOR ONE PROJECT, and the split is deliberate rather than untidy. All
# data goes through DATABASE_URL with node-postgres, because appending to the hash
# chain needs `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` inside a transaction and PostgREST - which is what
# the supabase-js client talks to - has no transactions at all. These two are for
# Storage only: the uploaded PDF bytes, which are files rather than rows.
# SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co

# THE SERVICE ROLE KEY IS A SECRET, AND IT IS THE STRONGEST ONE IN THE PROJECT: it
# bypasses row-level security and can read every row in every table. Server-side only.
#
# NEVER GIVE IT A VITE_ NAME to make it reachable from the frontend - see section 5.
# Vite compiles anything so prefixed into the client bundle, so `VITE_SUPABASE_SERVICE_
# ROLE_KEY` publishes it to everyone who loads the page, and this product's whole
# access boundary (who may open which case, who may read a position before the reveal)
# is enforced in the server process. A service-role key in a browser deletes it.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJ...

# Storage bucket for uploaded documents. Not a secret - it is a name, and the key above
# is what grants access to it - but it must exist and it must be PRIVATE: these are
# unpublished safety documents, and a public bucket serves them to anyone with the URL.
# Default `documents`.
# SUPABASE_BUCKET=documents

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. BROWSER-EXPOSED — anything named VITE_* IS PUBLIC
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Vite compiles these into the client bundle. They are readable by anyone who loads
# the page. Never put a credential, a key, or a project id behind a VITE_ name to
# "make it available to the frontend" — that publishes it.
#
# VITE_AUTO_PASSWORD is already public for exactly this reason. It signs everyone in
# as one demo identity, which means the record says that person decided whoever was
# at the keyboard (see the note in apps/deliberation/src/App.tsx). Fine for a demo,
# not fine as attribution.

# VITE_AUTO_EMAIL=r.okafor@arbiter.demo
# VITE_AUTO_PASSWORD=arbiter-demo-2026
# VITE_SITE_URL=/
# VITE_APP_URL=/deliberation/

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6. EVALUATION HARNESS — only for `npm run probe`
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PROBE_RUNS=20
# PROBE_CASE=data/probe-case.json
# PROBE_OUT=results/probe-runs.json
